1
00:00:00,532 --> 00:00:02,433
CHAPTER XL.

2
00:00:02,433 --> 00:00:06,565
ELIZABETH’S impatience to acquaint Jane with what had happened could no longer be

3
00:00:06,565 --> 00:00:11,933
overcome; and at length resolving to suppress every particular in which her sister was concerned,

4
00:00:11,933 --> 00:00:16,166
and preparing her to be surprised, she related to her the next morning the chief of the scene

5
00:00:16,166 --> 00:00:18,733
between Mr. Darcy and herself.

6
00:00:18,733 --> 00:00:23,133
Miss Bennet’s astonishment was soon lessened by the strong sisterly partiality which made

7
00:00:23,133 --> 00:00:28,099
any admiration of Elizabeth appear perfectly natural; and all surprise was shortly lost

8
00:00:28,099 --> 00:00:29,666
in other feelings.

9
00:00:29,666 --> 00:00:33,299
She was sorry that Mr. Darcy should have delivered his sentiments in a manner so little suited

10
00:00:33,299 --> 00:00:37,933
to recommend them; but still more was she grieved for the unhappiness which her sister’s

11
00:00:37,933 --> 00:00:39,200
refusal must have given him.

12
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:45,133
“His being so sure of succeeding was wrong,” said she, “and certainly ought not to have

13
00:00:45,133 --> 00:00:48,700
appeared; but consider how much it must increase his disappointment.”

14
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:54,466
“Indeed,” replied Elizabeth, “I am heartily sorry for him; but he has other feelings which

15
00:00:54,466 --> 00:00:57,700
will probably soon drive away his regard for me.

16
00:00:57,700 --> 00:01:00,000
You do not blame me, however, for refusing him?”

17
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,332
“Blame you!

18
00:01:01,332 --> 00:01:02,666
Oh, no.”

19
00:01:02,666 --> 00:01:06,599
“But you blame me for having spoken so warmly of Wickham?”

20
00:01:06,599 --> 00:01:10,000
“No—I do not know that you were wrong in saying what you did.”

21
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,400
“But you will know it, when I have told you what happened the very next day.”

22
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:19,299
She then spoke of the letter, repeating the whole of its contents as far as they concerned

23
00:01:19,299 --> 00:01:20,733
George Wickham.

24
00:01:20,733 --> 00:01:24,765
What a stroke was this for poor Jane, who would willingly have gone through the world

25
00:01:24,765 --> 00:01:29,099
without believing that so much wickedness existed in the whole race of mankind as was

26
00:01:29,099 --> 00:01:31,900
here collected in one individual!

27
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:36,433
Nor was Darcy’s vindication, though grateful to her feelings, capable of consoling her

28
00:01:36,433 --> 00:01:38,332
for such discovery.

29
00:01:38,332 --> 00:01:42,832
Most earnestly did she labour to prove the probability of error, and seek to clear one,

30
00:01:42,832 --> 00:01:44,265
without involving the other.

31
00:01:44,265 --> 00:01:49,500
“This will not do,” said Elizabeth; “you never will be able to make both of them good

32
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:50,666
for anything.

33
00:01:50,666 --> 00:01:54,099
Take your choice, but you must be satisfied with only one.

34
00:01:54,099 --> 00:01:58,765
There is but such a quantity of merit between them; just enough to make one good sort of

35
00:01:58,765 --> 00:02:01,765
man; and of late it has been shifting about pretty much.

36
00:02:01,765 --> 00:02:07,599
For my part, I am inclined to believe it all Mr. Darcy’s, but you shall do as you choose.”

37
00:02:07,599 --> 00:02:11,065
It was some time, however, before a smile could be extorted from Jane.

38
00:02:11,065 --> 00:02:14,900
“I do not know when I have been more shocked,” said she.

39
00:02:14,900 --> 00:02:16,765
“Wickham so very bad!

40
00:02:16,765 --> 00:02:18,832
It is almost past belief.

41
00:02:18,832 --> 00:02:20,966
And poor Mr. Darcy!

42
00:02:20,966 --> 00:02:24,733
dear Lizzy, only consider what he must have suffered.

43
00:02:24,733 --> 00:02:25,866
Such a disappointment!

44
00:02:25,866 --> 00:02:29,099
and with the knowledge of your ill opinion too!

45
00:02:29,099 --> 00:02:31,000
and having to relate such a thing of his sister!

46
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:36,200
It is really too distressing, I am sure you must feel it so.”

47
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:40,900
“Oh no, my regret and compassion are all done away by seeing you so full of both.

48
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:45,332
I know you will do him such ample justice, that I am growing every moment more unconcerned

49
00:02:45,332 --> 00:02:46,799
and indifferent.

50
00:02:46,799 --> 00:02:51,466
Your profusion makes me saving; and if you lament over him much longer, my heart will

51
00:02:51,466 --> 00:02:53,400
be as light as a feather.”

52
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:54,400
“Poor Wickham!

53
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:58,032
there is such an expression of goodness in his countenance!

54
00:02:58,032 --> 00:03:00,700
such an openness and gentleness in his manner.”

55
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:05,733
“There certainly was some great mismanagement in the education of those two young men.

56
00:03:05,733 --> 00:03:08,866
One has got all the goodness, and the other all the appearance of it.”

57
00:03:08,866 --> 00:03:13,633
“I never thought Mr. Darcy so deficient in the appearance of it as you used to do.”

58
00:03:13,633 --> 00:03:18,200
“And yet I meant to be uncommonly clever in taking so decided a dislike to him, without

59
00:03:18,200 --> 00:03:20,133
any reason.

60
00:03:20,133 --> 00:03:25,532
It is such a spur to one’s genius, such an opening for wit, to have a dislike of that

61
00:03:25,532 --> 00:03:26,532
kind.

62
00:03:26,532 --> 00:03:31,332
One may be continually abusive without saying anything just; but one cannot be always laughing

63
00:03:31,332 --> 00:03:34,565
at a man without now and then stumbling on something witty.”

64
00:03:34,565 --> 00:03:39,332
“Lizzy, when you first read that letter, I am sure you could not treat the matter as

65
00:03:39,332 --> 00:03:40,332
you do now.”

66
00:03:40,332 --> 00:03:42,700
“Indeed, I could not.

67
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:45,633
I was uncomfortable enough, I was very uncomfortable—I may say unhappy.

68
00:03:45,633 --> 00:03:51,633
And with no one to speak to of what I felt, no Jane to comfort me, and say that I had

69
00:03:51,633 --> 00:03:55,866
not been so very weak, and vain, and nonsensical, as I knew I had!

70
00:03:55,866 --> 00:03:58,400
Oh, how I wanted you!”

71
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:02,500
“How unfortunate that you should have used such very strong expressions in speaking of

72
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:07,565
Wickham to Mr. Darcy, for now they do appear wholly undeserved.”

73
00:04:07,565 --> 00:04:08,799
“Certainly.

74
00:04:08,799 --> 00:04:13,133
But the misfortune of speaking with bitterness is a most natural consequence of the prejudices

75
00:04:13,133 --> 00:04:15,032
I had been encouraging.

76
00:04:15,032 --> 00:04:17,032
There is one point on which I want your advice.

77
00:04:17,032 --> 00:04:22,900
I want to be told whether I ought, or ought not, to make our acquaintance in general understand

78
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:25,032
Wickham’s character.”

79
00:04:25,032 --> 00:04:29,966
Miss Bennet paused a little, and then replied, “Surely there can be no occasion for exposing

80
00:04:29,966 --> 00:04:31,032
him so dreadfully.

81
00:04:31,032 --> 00:04:33,332
What is your own opinion?”

82
00:04:33,332 --> 00:04:36,032
“That it ought not to be attempted.

83
00:04:36,032 --> 00:04:39,433
Mr. Darcy has not authorized me to make his communication public.

84
00:04:39,433 --> 00:04:43,332
On the contrary, every particular relative to his sister was meant to be kept as much

85
00:04:43,332 --> 00:04:47,966
as possible to myself; and if I endeavour to undeceive people as to the rest of his

86
00:04:47,966 --> 00:04:50,233
conduct, who will believe me?

87
00:04:50,233 --> 00:04:54,099
The general prejudice against Mr. Darcy is so violent, that it would be the death of

88
00:04:54,099 --> 00:04:58,299
half the good people in Meryton, to attempt to place him in an amiable light.

89
00:04:58,299 --> 00:04:59,700
I am not equal to it.

90
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:04,332
Wickham will soon be gone; and, therefore, it will not signify to anybody here what he

91
00:05:04,332 --> 00:05:05,332
really is.

92
00:05:05,332 --> 00:05:08,733
Some time hence it will be all found out, and then we may laugh at their stupidity in

93
00:05:08,733 --> 00:05:10,565
not knowing it before.

94
00:05:10,565 --> 00:05:12,966
At present I will say nothing about it.”

95
00:05:12,966 --> 00:05:14,599
“You are quite right.

96
00:05:14,599 --> 00:05:18,099
To have his errors made public might ruin him for ever.

97
00:05:18,099 --> 00:05:22,765
He is now, perhaps, sorry for what he has done, and anxious to re-establish a character.

98
00:05:22,765 --> 00:05:25,433
We must not make him desperate.”

99
00:05:25,433 --> 00:05:29,065
The tumult of Elizabeth’s mind was allayed by this conversation.

100
00:05:29,065 --> 00:05:32,733
She had got rid of two of the secrets which had weighed on her for a fortnight, and was

101
00:05:32,733 --> 00:05:37,700
certain of a willing listener in Jane, whenever she might wish to talk again of either.

102
00:05:37,700 --> 00:05:42,565
But there was still something lurking behind, of which prudence forbade the disclosure.

103
00:05:42,565 --> 00:05:46,900
She dared not relate the other half of Mr. Darcy’s letter, nor explain to her sister

104
00:05:46,900 --> 00:05:50,200
how sincerely she had been valued by his friend.

105
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:54,433
Here was knowledge in which no one could partake; and she was sensible that nothing less than

106
00:05:54,433 --> 00:05:58,733
a perfect understanding between the parties could justify her in throwing off this last

107
00:05:58,733 --> 00:06:00,099
encumbrance of mystery.

108
00:06:00,099 --> 00:06:05,866
“And then,” said she, “if that very improbable event should ever take place, I

109
00:06:05,866 --> 00:06:10,265
shall merely be able to tell what Bingley may tell in a much more agreeable manner himself.

110
00:06:10,265 --> 00:06:15,233
The liberty of communication cannot be mine till it has lost all its value!”

111
00:06:15,233 --> 00:06:19,966
She was now, on being settled at home, at leisure to observe the real state of her sister’s

112
00:06:19,966 --> 00:06:20,966
spirits.

113
00:06:20,966 --> 00:06:22,332
Jane was not happy.

114
00:06:22,332 --> 00:06:25,565
She still cherished a very tender affection for Bingley.

115
00:06:25,565 --> 00:06:29,733
Having never even fancied herself in love before, her regard had all the warmth of first

116
00:06:29,733 --> 00:06:34,832
attachment, and from her age and disposition, greater steadiness than first attachments

117
00:06:34,832 --> 00:06:40,366
often boast; and so fervently did she value his remembrance, and prefer him to every other

118
00:06:40,366 --> 00:06:44,400
man, that all her good sense, and all her attention to the feelings of her friends,

119
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:48,500
were requisite to check the indulgence of those regrets which must have been injurious

120
00:06:48,500 --> 00:06:50,599
to her own health and their tranquillity.

121
00:06:50,599 --> 00:06:56,332
“Well, Lizzy,” said Mrs. Bennet, one day, “what is your opinion now of this sad business

122
00:06:56,332 --> 00:06:57,332
of Jane’s?

123
00:06:57,332 --> 00:06:59,866
For my part, I am determined never to speak of it again to anybody.

124
00:06:59,866 --> 00:07:02,200
I told my sister Philips so the other day.

125
00:07:02,200 --> 00:07:05,433
But I cannot find out that Jane saw anything of him in London.

126
00:07:05,433 --> 00:07:10,400
Well, he is a very undeserving young man—and I do not suppose there is the least chance

127
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:12,099
in the world of her ever getting him now.

128
00:07:12,099 --> 00:07:15,866
There is no talk of his coming to Netherfield again in the summer; and I have inquired of

129
00:07:15,866 --> 00:07:17,366
everybody, too, who is likely to know.”

130
00:07:18,366 --> 00:07:21,299
“I am determined never to speak of it again” “I do not believe that he will ever live

131
00:07:21,299 --> 00:07:22,765
at Netherfield any more.”

132
00:07:22,765 --> 00:07:23,765
“Oh, well!

133
00:07:23,765 --> 00:07:24,799
it is just as he chooses.

134
00:07:24,799 --> 00:07:28,966
Nobody wants him to come; though I shall always say that he used my daughter extremely ill;

135
00:07:28,966 --> 00:07:32,233
and, if I was her, I would not have put up with it.

136
00:07:32,233 --> 00:07:36,200
Well, my comfort is, I am sure Jane will die of a broken heart, and then he will be sorry

137
00:07:36,200 --> 00:07:37,866
for what he has done.”

138
00:07:37,866 --> 00:07:42,500
But as Elizabeth could not receive comfort from any such expectation she made no answer.

139
00:07:42,500 --> 00:07:47,532
“Well, Lizzy,” continued her mother, soon afterwards, “and so the Collinses live very

140
00:07:47,532 --> 00:07:48,532
comfortable, do they?

141
00:07:48,532 --> 00:07:50,765
Well, well, I only hope it will last.

142
00:07:50,765 --> 00:07:52,866
And what sort of table do they keep?

143
00:07:52,866 --> 00:07:55,765
Charlotte is an excellent manager, I dare say.

144
00:07:55,765 --> 00:07:58,532
If she is half as sharp as her mother, she is saving enough.

145
00:07:58,532 --> 00:08:02,000
There is nothing extravagant in their housekeeping, I dare say.”

146
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:03,700
“No, nothing at all.”

147
00:08:03,700 --> 00:08:06,666
“A great deal of good management, depend upon it.

148
00:08:06,666 --> 00:08:07,666
Yes, yes.

149
00:08:07,666 --> 00:08:10,133
They will take care not to outrun their income.

150
00:08:10,133 --> 00:08:12,265
They will never be distressed for money.

151
00:08:12,265 --> 00:08:14,299
Well, much good may it do them!

152
00:08:14,299 --> 00:08:17,832
And so, I suppose, they often talk of having Longbourn when your father is dead.

153
00:08:17,832 --> 00:08:20,866
They look upon it quite as their own, I dare say, whenever that happens.”

154
00:08:20,866 --> 00:08:24,400
“It was a subject which they could not mention before me.”

155
00:08:24,400 --> 00:08:27,765
“No; it would have been strange if they had.

156
00:08:27,765 --> 00:08:31,966
But I make no doubt they often talk of it between themselves.

157
00:08:31,966 --> 00:08:36,733
Well, if they can be easy with an estate that is not lawfully their own, so much the better.

158
00:08:36,733 --> 00:08:40,532
I should be ashamed of having one that was only entailed on me.”

159
00:08:42,799 --> 00:08:48,433
“When Colonel Miller’s regiment went.”

160
00:08:48,433 --> 00:08:50,933
CHAPTER XLI.

161
00:08:50,933 --> 00:08:53,500
THE first week of their return was soon gone.

162
00:08:53,500 --> 00:08:55,065
The second began.

163
00:08:55,065 --> 00:08:59,500
It was the last of the regiment’s stay in Meryton, and all the young ladies in the neighbourhood

164
00:08:59,500 --> 00:09:01,233
were drooping apace.

165
00:09:01,233 --> 00:09:03,933
The dejection was almost universal.

166
00:09:03,933 --> 00:09:08,832
The elder Miss Bennets alone were still able to eat, drink, and sleep, and pursue the usual

167
00:09:08,832 --> 00:09:11,000
course of their employments.

168
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:15,733
Very frequently were they reproached for this insensibility by Kitty and Lydia, whose own

169
00:09:15,733 --> 00:09:21,133
misery was extreme, and who could not comprehend such hard-heartedness in any of the family.

170
00:09:21,133 --> 00:09:23,065
“Good Heaven!

171
00:09:23,065 --> 00:09:24,299
What is to become of us?

172
00:09:24,299 --> 00:09:25,832
What are we to do?”

173
00:09:25,832 --> 00:09:28,866
would they often exclaim in the bitterness of woe.

174
00:09:28,866 --> 00:09:32,133
“How can you be smiling so, Lizzy?”

175
00:09:32,133 --> 00:09:35,900
Their affectionate mother shared all their grief; she remembered what she had herself

176
00:09:35,900 --> 00:09:39,133
endured on a similar occasion five-and-twenty years ago.

177
00:09:39,133 --> 00:09:45,400
“I am sure,” said she, “I cried for two days together when Colonel Miller’s

178
00:09:45,400 --> 00:09:46,400
regiment went away.

179
00:09:46,400 --> 00:09:49,166
I thought I should have broke my heart.”

180
00:09:49,166 --> 00:09:52,733
“I am sure I shall break mine,” said Lydia.

181
00:09:52,733 --> 00:09:55,966
“If one could but go to Brighton!”

182
00:09:55,966 --> 00:09:56,966
observed Mrs. Bennet.

183
00:09:56,966 --> 00:09:59,966
“Oh yes!—if one could but go to Brighton!

184
00:09:59,966 --> 00:10:02,400
But papa is so disagreeable.”

185
00:10:02,400 --> 00:10:06,000
“A little sea-bathing would set me up for ever.”

186
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:12,233
“And my aunt Philips is sure it would do me a great deal of good,” added Kitty.

187
00:10:12,233 --> 00:10:17,000
Such were the kind of lamentations resounding perpetually through Longbourn House.

188
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:21,966
Elizabeth tried to be diverted by them; but all sense of pleasure was lost in shame.

189
00:10:21,966 --> 00:10:26,200
She felt anew the justice of Mr. Darcy’s objections; and never had she before been

190
00:10:26,200 --> 00:10:30,900
so much disposed to pardon his interference in the views of his friend.

191
00:10:30,900 --> 00:10:35,700
But the gloom of Lydia’s prospect was shortly cleared away; for she received an invitation

192
00:10:35,700 --> 00:10:41,133
from Mrs. Forster, the wife of the colonel of the regiment, to accompany her to Brighton.

193
00:10:41,133 --> 00:10:45,099
This invaluable friend was a very young woman, and very lately married.

194
00:10:45,099 --> 00:10:49,866
A resemblance in good-humour and good spirits had recommended her and Lydia to each other,

195
00:10:49,866 --> 00:10:54,233
and out of their three months’ acquaintance they had been intimate two.

196
00:10:54,233 --> 00:10:58,765
The rapture of Lydia on this occasion, her adoration of Mrs. Forster, the delight of

197
00:10:58,765 --> 00:11:03,599
Mrs. Bennet, and the mortification of Kitty, are scarcely to be described.

198
00:11:03,599 --> 00:11:09,065
Wholly inattentive to her sister’s feelings, Lydia flew about the house in restless ecstasy,

199
00:11:09,065 --> 00:11:13,133
calling for everyone’s congratulations, and laughing and talking with more violence

200
00:11:13,133 --> 00:11:18,032
than ever; whilst the luckless Kitty continued in the parlour repining at her fate in terms

201
00:11:18,032 --> 00:11:21,265
as unreasonable as her accent was peevish.

202
00:11:21,265 --> 00:11:26,733
“I cannot see why Mrs. Forster should not ask me as well as Lydia,” said she, “though

203
00:11:26,733 --> 00:11:28,900
I am not her particular friend.

204
00:11:28,900 --> 00:11:35,233
I have just as much right to be asked as she has, and more too, for I am two years older.”

205
00:11:35,233 --> 00:11:39,933
In vain did Elizabeth attempt to make her reasonable, and Jane to make her resigned.

206
00:11:39,933 --> 00:11:45,065
As for Elizabeth herself, this invitation was so far from exciting in her the same feelings

207
00:11:45,065 --> 00:11:49,265
as in her mother and Lydia, that she considered it as the death-warrant of all possibility

208
00:11:49,265 --> 00:11:54,433
of common sense for the latter; and detestable as such a step must make her, were it known,

209
00:11:54,433 --> 00:11:58,565
she could not help secretly advising her father not to let her go.

210
00:11:58,565 --> 00:12:02,933
She represented to him all the improprieties of Lydia’s general behaviour, the little

211
00:12:02,933 --> 00:12:07,565
advantage she could derive from the friendship of such a woman as Mrs. Forster, and the probability

212
00:12:07,565 --> 00:12:11,966
of her being yet more imprudent with such a companion at Brighton, where the temptations

213
00:12:11,966 --> 00:12:14,099
must be greater than at home.

214
00:12:14,099 --> 00:12:19,733
He heard her attentively, and then said,— “Lydia will never be easy till she has exposed

215
00:12:19,733 --> 00:12:24,633
herself in some public place or other, and we can never expect her to do it with so little

216
00:12:24,633 --> 00:12:27,832
expense or inconvenience to her family as under the present circumstances.”

217
00:12:27,832 --> 00:12:34,332
“If you were aware,” said Elizabeth, “of the very great disadvantage to us all, which

218
00:12:34,332 --> 00:12:39,633
must arise from the public notice of Lydia’s unguarded and imprudent manner, nay, which

219
00:12:39,633 --> 00:12:44,000
has already arisen from it, I am sure you would judge differently in the affair.”

220
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:45,966
“Already arisen!”

221
00:12:45,966 --> 00:12:47,166
repeated Mr. Bennet.

222
00:12:47,166 --> 00:12:48,166
“What!

223
00:12:48,166 --> 00:12:50,565
has she frightened away some of your lovers?

224
00:12:50,565 --> 00:12:51,765
Poor little Lizzy!

225
00:12:51,765 --> 00:12:54,433
But do not be cast down.

226
00:12:54,433 --> 00:12:58,633
Such squeamish youths as cannot bear to be connected with a little absurdity are not

227
00:12:58,633 --> 00:13:00,466
worth a regret.

228
00:13:00,466 --> 00:13:06,332
Come, let me see the list of the pitiful fellows who have been kept aloof by Lydia’s folly.”

229
00:13:06,332 --> 00:13:08,032
“Indeed, you are mistaken.

230
00:13:08,032 --> 00:13:10,633
I have no such injuries to resent.

231
00:13:10,633 --> 00:13:14,500
It is not of peculiar, but of general evils, which I am now complaining.

232
00:13:14,500 --> 00:13:20,400
Our importance, our respectability in the world, must be affected by the wild volatility,

233
00:13:20,400 --> 00:13:25,700
the assurance and disdain of all restraint which mark Lydia’s character.

234
00:13:25,700 --> 00:13:28,366
Excuse me,—for I must speak plainly.

235
00:13:28,366 --> 00:13:32,832
If you, my dear father, will not take the trouble of checking her exuberant spirits,

236
00:13:32,832 --> 00:13:37,233
and of teaching her that her present pursuits are not to be the business of her life, she

237
00:13:37,233 --> 00:13:40,166
will soon be beyond the reach of amendment.

238
00:13:40,166 --> 00:13:45,032
Her character will be fixed; and she will, at sixteen, be the most determined flirt that

239
00:13:45,032 --> 00:13:50,000
ever made herself and her family ridiculous;—a flirt, too, in the worst and meanest degree

240
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:55,466
of flirtation; without any attraction beyond youth and a tolerable person; and, from the

241
00:13:55,466 --> 00:14:00,200
ignorance and emptiness of her mind, wholly unable to ward off any portion of that universal

242
00:14:00,200 --> 00:14:03,966
contempt which her rage for admiration will excite.

243
00:14:03,966 --> 00:14:06,933
In this danger Kitty is also comprehended.

244
00:14:06,933 --> 00:14:08,633
She will follow wherever Lydia leads.

245
00:14:08,633 --> 00:14:12,099
Vain, ignorant, idle, and absolutely uncontrolled!

246
00:14:12,099 --> 00:14:17,532
Oh, my dear father, can you suppose it possible that they will not be censured and despised

247
00:14:17,532 --> 00:14:23,233
wherever they are known, and that their sisters will not be often involved in the disgrace?”

248
00:14:23,233 --> 00:14:27,466
Mr. Bennet saw that her whole heart was in the subject; and, affectionately taking her

249
00:14:27,466 --> 00:14:31,866
hand, said, in reply,— “Do not make yourself uneasy, my love.

250
00:14:31,866 --> 00:14:36,733
Wherever you and Jane are known, you must be respected and valued; and you will not

251
00:14:36,733 --> 00:14:43,233
appear to less advantage for having a couple of—or I may say, three—very silly sisters.

252
00:14:43,233 --> 00:14:47,200
We shall have no peace at Longbourn if Lydia does not go to Brighton.

253
00:14:47,200 --> 00:14:49,133
Let her go, then.

254
00:14:49,133 --> 00:14:53,433
Colonel Forster is a sensible man, and will keep her out of any real mischief; and she

255
00:14:53,433 --> 00:14:57,032
is luckily too poor to be an object of prey to anybody.

256
00:14:57,032 --> 00:15:01,532
At Brighton she will be of less importance even as a common flirt than she has been here.

257
00:15:01,532 --> 00:15:04,200
The officers will find women better worth their notice.

258
00:15:04,200 --> 00:15:09,099
Let us hope, therefore, that her being there may teach her her own insignificance.

259
00:15:09,099 --> 00:15:13,466
At any rate, she cannot grow many degrees worse, without authorizing us to lock her

260
00:15:13,466 --> 00:15:15,166
up for the rest of her life.”

261
00:15:15,166 --> 00:15:19,933
With this answer Elizabeth was forced to be content; but her own opinion continued the

262
00:15:19,933 --> 00:15:23,265
same, and she left him disappointed and sorry.

263
00:15:23,265 --> 00:15:27,133
It was not in her nature, however, to increase her vexations by dwelling on them.

264
00:15:27,133 --> 00:15:31,966
She was confident of having performed her duty; and to fret over unavoidable evils,

265
00:15:31,966 --> 00:15:36,966
or augment them by anxiety, was no part of her disposition.

266
00:15:36,966 --> 00:15:41,099
Had Lydia and her mother known the substance of her conference with her father, their indignation

267
00:15:41,099 --> 00:15:44,565
would hardly have found expression in their united volubility.

268
00:15:44,565 --> 00:15:50,366
In Lydia’s imagination, a visit to Brighton comprised every possibility of earthly happiness.

269
00:15:50,366 --> 00:15:55,233
She saw, with the creative eye of fancy, the streets of that gay bathing-place covered

270
00:15:55,233 --> 00:15:56,532
with officers.

271
00:15:56,532 --> 00:16:02,133
She saw herself the object of attention to tens and to scores of them at present unknown.

272
00:16:02,133 --> 00:16:07,332
She saw all the glories of the camp: its tents stretched forth in beauteous uniformity of

273
00:16:07,332 --> 00:16:12,166
lines, crowded with the young and the gay, and dazzling with scarlet; and, to complete

274
00:16:12,166 --> 00:16:17,032
the view, she saw herself seated beneath a tent, tenderly flirting with at least six

275
00:16:17,032 --> 00:16:18,032
officers at once.

276
00:16:20,032 --> 00:16:23,633
Had she known that her sister sought to tear her from such prospects and such realities

277
00:16:23,633 --> 00:16:26,532
as these, what would have been her sensations?

278
00:16:26,532 --> 00:16:30,933
They could have been understood only by her mother, who might have felt nearly the same.

279
00:16:30,933 --> 00:16:35,666
Lydia’s going to Brighton was all that consoled her for the melancholy conviction of her husband’s

280
00:16:35,666 --> 00:16:37,733
never intending to go there himself.

281
00:16:37,733 --> 00:16:42,733
But they were entirely ignorant of what had passed; and their raptures continued, with

282
00:16:42,733 --> 00:16:47,200
little intermission, to the very day of Lydia’s leaving home.

283
00:16:47,200 --> 00:16:50,233
Elizabeth was now to see Mr. Wickham for the last time.

284
00:16:50,233 --> 00:16:54,500
Having been frequently in company with him since her return, agitation was pretty well

285
00:16:54,500 --> 00:16:58,565
over; the agitations of former partiality entirely so.

286
00:16:58,565 --> 00:17:02,599
She had even learnt to detect, in the very gentleness which had first delighted her,

287
00:17:02,599 --> 00:17:06,265
an affectation and a sameness to disgust and weary.

288
00:17:06,265 --> 00:17:11,166
In his present behaviour to herself, moreover, she had a fresh source of displeasure; for

289
00:17:11,166 --> 00:17:15,032
the inclination he soon testified of renewing those attentions which had marked the early

290
00:17:15,032 --> 00:17:20,500
part of their acquaintance could only serve, after what had since passed, to provoke her.

291
00:17:20,500 --> 00:17:25,733
She lost all concern for him in finding herself thus selected as the object of such idle and

292
00:17:25,733 --> 00:17:30,400
frivolous gallantry; and while she steadily repressed it, could not but feel the reproof

293
00:17:30,400 --> 00:17:35,400
contained in his believing, that however long, and for whatever cause, his attentions had

294
00:17:35,400 --> 00:17:40,200
been withdrawn, her vanity would be gratified, and her preference secured, at any time, by

295
00:17:40,200 --> 00:17:41,700
their renewal.

296
00:17:41,700 --> 00:17:45,933
On the very last day of the regiment’s remaining in Meryton, he dined, with others of the officers,

297
00:17:45,933 --> 00:17:50,065
at Longbourn; and so little was Elizabeth disposed to part from him in good-humour,

298
00:17:50,065 --> 00:17:54,900
that, on his making some inquiry as to the manner in which her time had passed at Hunsford,

299
00:17:54,900 --> 00:17:58,466
she mentioned Colonel Fitzwilliam’s and Mr. Darcy’s having both spent three weeks

300
00:17:58,466 --> 00:18:02,433
at Rosings, and asked him if he were acquainted with the former.

301
00:18:02,433 --> 00:18:07,599
He looked surprised, displeased, alarmed; but, with a moment’s recollection, and a

302
00:18:07,599 --> 00:18:12,065
returning smile, replied, that he had formerly seen him often; and, after observing that

303
00:18:12,065 --> 00:18:15,765
he was a very gentlemanlike man, asked her how she had liked him.

304
00:18:15,765 --> 00:18:17,400
Her answer was warmly in his favour.

305
00:18:17,400 --> 00:18:22,400
With an air of indifference, he soon afterwards added, “How long did you say that he was

306
00:18:22,400 --> 00:18:23,400
at Rosings?”

307
00:18:23,400 --> 00:18:25,099
“Nearly three weeks.”

308
00:18:25,099 --> 00:18:27,200
“And you saw him frequently?”

309
00:18:27,200 --> 00:18:29,099
“Yes, almost every day.”

310
00:18:29,099 --> 00:18:32,799
“His manners are very different from his cousin’s.”

311
00:18:32,799 --> 00:18:38,866
“Yes, very different; but I think Mr. Darcy improves on acquaintance.”

312
00:18:38,866 --> 00:18:40,000
“Indeed!”

313
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,666
cried Wickham, with a look which did not escape her.

314
00:18:42,666 --> 00:18:49,265
“And pray may I ask—” but checking himself, he added, in a gayer tone, “Is it in address

315
00:18:49,265 --> 00:18:50,433
that he improves?

316
00:18:50,433 --> 00:18:56,065
Has he deigned to add aught of civility to his ordinary style? for I dare not hope,”

317
00:18:56,065 --> 00:19:00,700
he continued, in a lower and more serious tone, “that he is improved in essentials.”

318
00:19:00,700 --> 00:19:03,133
“Oh, no!” said Elizabeth.

319
00:19:03,133 --> 00:19:07,866
“In essentials, I believe, he is very much what he ever was.”

320
00:19:07,866 --> 00:19:12,366
While she spoke, Wickham looked as if scarcely knowing whether to rejoice over her words

321
00:19:12,366 --> 00:19:14,366
or to distrust their meaning.

322
00:19:14,366 --> 00:19:18,532
There was a something in her countenance which made him listen with an apprehensive and anxious

323
00:19:18,532 --> 00:19:23,265
attention, while she added,— “When I said that he improved on acquaintance,

324
00:19:23,265 --> 00:19:27,866
I did not mean that either his mind or manners were in a state of improvement; but that,

325
00:19:27,866 --> 00:19:31,433
from knowing him better, his disposition was better understood.”

326
00:19:31,433 --> 00:19:37,265
Wickham’s alarm now appeared in a heightened complexion and agitated look; for a few minutes

327
00:19:37,265 --> 00:19:42,565
he was silent; till, shaking off his embarrassment, he turned to her again, and said in the gentlest

328
00:19:42,565 --> 00:19:46,966
of accents,— “You, who so well know my feelings towards

329
00:19:46,966 --> 00:19:52,433
Mr. Darcy, will readily comprehend how sincerely I must rejoice that he is wise enough to assume

330
00:19:52,433 --> 00:19:54,900
even the appearance of what is right.

331
00:19:54,900 --> 00:20:00,933
His pride, in that direction, may be of service, if not to himself, to many others, for it

332
00:20:00,933 --> 00:20:04,832
must deter him from such foul misconduct as I have suffered by.

333
00:20:04,832 --> 00:20:10,000
I only fear that the sort of cautiousness to which you, I imagine, have been alluding,

334
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:14,832
is merely adopted on his visits to his aunt, of whose good opinion and judgment he stands

335
00:20:14,832 --> 00:20:16,233
much in awe.

336
00:20:16,233 --> 00:20:20,933
His fear of her has always operated, I know, when they were together; and a good deal is

337
00:20:20,933 --> 00:20:25,366
to be imputed to his wish of forwarding the match with Miss de Bourgh, which I am certain

338
00:20:25,366 --> 00:20:27,799
he has very much at heart.”

339
00:20:27,799 --> 00:20:31,933
Elizabeth could not repress a smile at this, but she answered only by a slight inclination

340
00:20:31,933 --> 00:20:32,966
of the head.

341
00:20:32,966 --> 00:20:37,500
She saw that he wanted to engage her on the old subject of his grievances, and she was

342
00:20:37,500 --> 00:20:39,366
in no humour to indulge him.

343
00:20:39,366 --> 00:20:44,265
The rest of the evening passed with the appearance, on his side, of usual cheerfulness, but with

344
00:20:44,265 --> 00:20:49,433
no further attempt to distinguish Elizabeth; and they parted at last with mutual civility,

345
00:20:49,433 --> 00:20:53,866
and possibly a mutual desire of never meeting again.

346
00:20:53,866 --> 00:20:58,032
When the party broke up, Lydia returned with Mrs. Forster to Meryton, from whence they

347
00:20:58,032 --> 00:21:00,233
were to set out early the next morning.

348
00:21:00,233 --> 00:21:03,733
The separation between her and her family was rather noisy than pathetic.

349
00:21:03,733 --> 00:21:09,433
Kitty was the only one who shed tears; but she did weep from vexation and envy.

350
00:21:09,433 --> 00:21:13,565
Mrs. Bennet was diffuse in her good wishes for the felicity of her daughter, and impressive

351
00:21:13,565 --> 00:21:17,200
in her injunctions that she would not miss the opportunity of enjoying herself as much

352
00:21:17,200 --> 00:21:22,065
as possible,—advice which there was every reason to believe would be attended to; and,

353
00:21:22,065 --> 00:21:26,233
in the clamorous happiness of Lydia herself in bidding farewell, the more gentle adieus

354
00:21:26,233 --> 00:21:28,832
of her sisters were uttered without being heard.

355
00:21:29,832 --> 00:21:37,166
“The arrival of the Pardiners.”

356
00:21:37,166 --> 00:21:40,000
CHAPTER XLII.

357
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:43,933
HAD Elizabeth’s opinion been all drawn from her own family, she could not have formed

358
00:21:43,933 --> 00:21:48,133
a very pleasing picture of conjugal felicity or domestic comfort.

359
00:21:48,133 --> 00:21:52,733
Her father, captivated by youth and beauty, and that appearance of good-humour which youth

360
00:21:52,733 --> 00:21:57,565
and beauty generally give, had married a woman whose weak understanding and illiberal mind

361
00:21:57,565 --> 00:22:01,633
had very early in their marriage put an end to all real affection for her.

362
00:22:01,633 --> 00:22:07,133
Respect, esteem, and confidence had vanished for ever; and all his views of domestic happiness

363
00:22:07,133 --> 00:22:08,265
were overthrown.

364
00:22:08,265 --> 00:22:12,700
But Mr. Bennet was not of a disposition to seek comfort for the disappointment which

365
00:22:12,700 --> 00:22:17,233
his own imprudence had brought on in any of those pleasures which too often console the

366
00:22:17,233 --> 00:22:20,133
unfortunate for their folly or their vice.

367
00:22:20,133 --> 00:22:25,065
He was fond of the country and of books; and from these tastes had arisen his principal

368
00:22:25,065 --> 00:22:26,166
enjoyments.

369
00:22:26,166 --> 00:22:31,666
To his wife he was very little otherwise indebted than as her ignorance and folly had contributed

370
00:22:31,666 --> 00:22:33,400
to his amusement.

371
00:22:33,400 --> 00:22:37,133
This is not the sort of happiness which a man would in general wish to owe to his wife;

372
00:22:37,133 --> 00:22:41,633
but where other powers of entertainment are wanting, the true philosopher will derive

373
00:22:41,633 --> 00:22:44,099
benefit from such as are given.

374
00:22:44,099 --> 00:22:48,599
Elizabeth, however, had never been blind to the impropriety of her father’s behaviour

375
00:22:48,599 --> 00:22:49,765
as a husband.

376
00:22:49,765 --> 00:22:54,900
She had always seen it with pain; but respecting his abilities, and grateful for his affectionate

377
00:22:54,900 --> 00:22:59,332
treatment of herself, she endeavoured to forget what she could not overlook, and to banish

378
00:22:59,332 --> 00:23:04,000
from her thoughts that continual breach of conjugal obligation and decorum which, in

379
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:08,065
exposing his wife to the contempt of her own children, was so highly reprehensible.

380
00:23:08,065 --> 00:23:13,332
But she had never felt so strongly as now the disadvantages which must attend the children

381
00:23:13,332 --> 00:23:18,000
of so unsuitable a marriage, nor ever been so fully aware of the evils arising from so

382
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:24,200
ill-judged a direction of talents—talents which, rightly used, might at least have preserved

383
00:23:24,200 --> 00:23:28,666
the respectability of his daughters, even if incapable of enlarging the mind of his

384
00:23:28,666 --> 00:23:30,233
wife.

385
00:23:30,233 --> 00:23:34,133
When Elizabeth had rejoiced over Wickham’s departure, she found little other cause for

386
00:23:34,133 --> 00:23:36,866
satisfaction in the loss of the regiment.

387
00:23:36,866 --> 00:23:41,866
Their parties abroad were less varied than before; and at home she had a mother and sister,

388
00:23:41,866 --> 00:23:46,000
whose constant repinings at the dulness of everything around them threw a real gloom

389
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:51,032
over their domestic circle; and, though Kitty might in time regain her natural degree of

390
00:23:51,032 --> 00:23:56,032
sense, since the disturbers of her brain were removed, her other sister, from whose disposition

391
00:23:56,032 --> 00:24:01,200
greater evil might be apprehended, was likely to be hardened in all her folly and assurance,

392
00:24:01,200 --> 00:24:06,133
by a situation of such double danger as a watering-place and a camp.

393
00:24:06,133 --> 00:24:11,233
Upon the whole, therefore, she found, what has been sometimes found before, that an event

394
00:24:11,233 --> 00:24:16,532
to which she had looked forward with impatient desire, did not, in taking place, bring all

395
00:24:16,532 --> 00:24:19,200
the satisfaction she had promised herself.

396
00:24:19,200 --> 00:24:23,466
It was consequently necessary to name some other period for the commencement of actual

397
00:24:23,466 --> 00:24:28,233
felicity; to have some other point on which her wishes and hopes might be fixed, and by

398
00:24:28,233 --> 00:24:33,633
again enjoying the pleasure of anticipation, console herself for the present, and prepare

399
00:24:33,633 --> 00:24:35,666
for another disappointment.

400
00:24:35,666 --> 00:24:40,900
Her tour to the Lakes was now the object of her happiest thoughts: it was her best consolation

401
00:24:40,900 --> 00:24:44,900
for all the uncomfortable hours which the discontentedness of her mother and Kitty made

402
00:24:44,900 --> 00:24:49,832
inevitable; and could she have included Jane in the scheme, every part of it would have

403
00:24:49,832 --> 00:24:50,866
been perfect.

404
00:24:50,866 --> 00:24:55,233
“But it is fortunate,” thought she, “that I have something to wish for.

405
00:24:55,233 --> 00:24:59,832
Were the whole arrangement complete, my disappointment would be certain.

406
00:24:59,832 --> 00:25:05,466
But here, by carrying with me one ceaseless source of regret in my sister’s absence,

407
00:25:05,466 --> 00:25:08,765
I may reasonably hope to have all my expectations of pleasure realized.

408
00:25:08,765 --> 00:25:15,166
A scheme of which every part promises delight can never be successful; and general disappointment

409
00:25:15,166 --> 00:25:20,400
is only warded off by the defence of some little peculiar vexation.”

410
00:25:20,400 --> 00:25:24,366
When Lydia went away she promised to write very often and very minutely to her mother

411
00:25:24,366 --> 00:25:29,299
and Kitty; but her letters were always long expected, and always very short.

412
00:25:29,299 --> 00:25:32,700
Those to her mother contained little else than that they were just returned from the

413
00:25:32,700 --> 00:25:36,666
library, where such and such officers had attended them, and where she had seen such

414
00:25:36,666 --> 00:25:41,765
beautiful ornaments as made her quite wild; that she had a new gown, or a new parasol,

415
00:25:41,765 --> 00:25:45,400
which she would have described more fully, but was obliged to leave off in a violent

416
00:25:45,400 --> 00:25:49,599
hurry, as Mrs. Forster called her, and they were going to the camp; and from her correspondence

417
00:25:49,599 --> 00:25:54,466
with her sister there was still less to be learnt, for her letters to Kitty, though rather

418
00:25:54,466 --> 00:25:59,166
longer, were much too full of lines under the words to be made public.

419
00:25:59,166 --> 00:26:03,866
After the first fortnight or three weeks of her absence, health, good-humour, and cheerfulness

420
00:26:03,866 --> 00:26:06,299
began to reappear at Longbourn.

421
00:26:06,299 --> 00:26:08,099
Everything wore a happier aspect.

422
00:26:08,099 --> 00:26:11,765
The families who had been in town for the winter came back again, and summer finery

423
00:26:11,765 --> 00:26:13,966
and summer engagements arose.

424
00:26:13,966 --> 00:26:18,799
Mrs. Bennet was restored to her usual querulous serenity; and by the middle of June Kitty

425
00:26:18,799 --> 00:26:23,133
was so much recovered as to be able to enter Meryton without tears,—an event of such

426
00:26:23,133 --> 00:26:27,500
happy promise as to make Elizabeth hope, that by the following Christmas she might be so

427
00:26:27,500 --> 00:26:31,832
tolerably reasonable as not to mention an officer above once a day, unless, by some

428
00:26:31,832 --> 00:26:35,733
cruel and malicious arrangement at the War Office, another regiment should be quartered

429
00:26:35,733 --> 00:26:37,400
in Meryton.

430
00:26:37,400 --> 00:26:41,433
The time fixed for the beginning of their northern tour was now fast approaching; and

431
00:26:41,433 --> 00:26:45,765
a fortnight only was wanting of it, when a letter arrived from Mrs. Gardiner, which at

432
00:26:45,765 --> 00:26:49,565
once delayed its commencement and curtailed its extent.

433
00:26:49,565 --> 00:26:53,765
Mr. Gardiner would be prevented by business from setting out till a fortnight later in

434
00:26:53,765 --> 00:26:58,099
July, and must be in London again within a month; and as that left too short a period

435
00:26:58,099 --> 00:27:02,532
for them to go so far, and see so much as they had proposed, or at least to see it with

436
00:27:02,532 --> 00:27:06,900
the leisure and comfort they had built on, they were obliged to give up the Lakes, and

437
00:27:06,900 --> 00:27:11,633
substitute a more contracted tour; and, according to the present plan, were to go no farther

438
00:27:11,633 --> 00:27:13,233
northward than Derbyshire.

439
00:27:13,233 --> 00:27:17,832
In that county there was enough to be seen to occupy the chief of their three weeks;

440
00:27:17,832 --> 00:27:21,299
and to Mrs. Gardiner it had a peculiarly strong attraction.

441
00:27:21,299 --> 00:27:24,933
The town where she had formerly passed some years of her life, and where they were now

442
00:27:24,933 --> 00:27:29,466
to spend a few days, was probably as great an object of her curiosity as all the celebrated

443
00:27:29,466 --> 00:27:33,933
beauties of Matlock, Chatsworth, Dovedale, or the Peak.

444
00:27:33,933 --> 00:27:38,666
Elizabeth was excessively disappointed: she had set her heart on seeing the Lakes; and

445
00:27:38,666 --> 00:27:41,133
still thought there might have been time enough.

446
00:27:41,133 --> 00:27:45,565
But it was her business to be satisfied—and certainly her temper to be happy; and all

447
00:27:45,565 --> 00:27:47,565
was soon right again.

448
00:27:47,565 --> 00:27:50,599
With the mention of Derbyshire, there were many ideas connected.

449
00:27:50,599 --> 00:27:54,666
It was impossible for her to see the word without thinking of Pemberley and its owner.

450
00:27:54,666 --> 00:28:00,666
“But surely,” said she, “I may enter his county with impunity, and rob it of a

451
00:28:00,666 --> 00:28:04,332
few petrified spars, without his perceiving me.”

452
00:28:04,332 --> 00:28:07,065
The period of expectation was now doubled.

453
00:28:07,065 --> 00:28:09,966
Four weeks were to pass away before her uncle and aunt’s arrival.

454
00:28:09,966 --> 00:28:15,433
But they did pass away, and Mr. and Mrs. Gardiner, with their four children, did at length appear

455
00:28:15,433 --> 00:28:16,733
at Longbourn.

456
00:28:16,733 --> 00:28:21,599
The children, two girls of six and eight years old, and two younger boys, were to be left

457
00:28:21,599 --> 00:28:25,900
under the particular care of their cousin Jane, who was the general favourite, and whose

458
00:28:25,900 --> 00:28:29,900
steady sense and sweetness of temper exactly adapted her for attending to them in every

459
00:28:29,900 --> 00:28:34,265
way—teaching them, playing with them, and loving them.

460
00:28:34,265 --> 00:28:38,565
The Gardiners stayed only one night at Longbourn, and set off the next morning with Elizabeth

461
00:28:38,565 --> 00:28:41,200
in pursuit of novelty and amusement.

462
00:28:41,200 --> 00:28:46,099
One enjoyment was certain—that of suitableness as companions; a suitableness which comprehended

463
00:28:46,099 --> 00:28:50,933
health and temper to bear inconveniences—cheerfulness to enhance every pleasure—and affection

464
00:28:50,933 --> 00:28:56,433
and intelligence, which might supply it among themselves if there were disappointments abroad.

465
00:28:56,433 --> 00:29:00,666
It is not the object of this work to give a description of Derbyshire, nor of any of

466
00:29:00,666 --> 00:29:05,166
the remarkable places through which their route thither lay—Oxford, Blenheim, Warwick,

467
00:29:05,166 --> 00:29:09,099
Kenilworth, Birmingham, etc., are sufficiently known.

468
00:29:09,099 --> 00:29:12,366
A small part of Derbyshire is all the present concern.

469
00:29:12,366 --> 00:29:16,433
To the little town of Lambton, the scene of Mrs. Gardiner’s former residence, and where

470
00:29:16,433 --> 00:29:20,765
she had lately learned that some acquaintance still remained, they bent their steps, after

471
00:29:20,765 --> 00:29:25,832
having seen all the principal wonders of the country; and within five miles of Lambton,

472
00:29:25,832 --> 00:29:28,866
Elizabeth found, from her aunt, that Pemberley was situated.

473
00:29:28,866 --> 00:29:32,666
It was not in their direct road; nor more than a mile or two out of it.

474
00:29:32,666 --> 00:29:36,933
In talking over their route the evening before, Mrs. Gardiner expressed an inclination to

475
00:29:36,933 --> 00:29:38,700
see the place again.

476
00:29:38,700 --> 00:29:42,966
Mr. Gardiner declared his willingness, and Elizabeth was applied to for her approbation.

477
00:29:42,966 --> 00:29:48,133
“My love, should not you like to see a place of which you have heard so much?” said her

478
00:29:48,133 --> 00:29:49,133
aunt.

479
00:29:49,133 --> 00:29:52,265
“A place, too, with which so many of your acquaintance are connected.

480
00:29:52,265 --> 00:29:55,000
Wickham passed all his youth there, you know.”

481
00:29:55,000 --> 00:29:56,765
Elizabeth was distressed.

482
00:29:56,765 --> 00:30:00,933
She felt that she had no business at Pemberley, and was obliged to assume a disinclination

483
00:30:00,933 --> 00:30:02,200
for seeing it.

484
00:30:02,200 --> 00:30:07,000
She must own that she was tired of great houses: after going over so many, she really had no

485
00:30:07,000 --> 00:30:10,065
pleasure in fine carpets or satin curtains.

486
00:30:10,065 --> 00:30:11,700
Mrs. Gardiner abused her stupidity.

487
00:30:11,700 --> 00:30:17,700
“If it were merely a fine house richly furnished,” said she, “I should not care about it myself;

488
00:30:17,700 --> 00:30:20,032
but the grounds are delightful.

489
00:30:20,032 --> 00:30:23,666
They have some of the finest woods in the country.”

490
00:30:23,666 --> 00:30:27,133
Elizabeth said no more; but her mind could not acquiesce.

491
00:30:27,133 --> 00:30:30,700
The possibility of meeting Mr. Darcy, while viewing the place, instantly occurred.

492
00:30:30,700 --> 00:30:32,799
It would be dreadful!

493
00:30:32,799 --> 00:30:36,733
She blushed at the very idea; and thought it would be better to speak openly to her

494
00:30:36,733 --> 00:30:38,700
aunt, than to run such a risk.

495
00:30:38,700 --> 00:30:42,400
But against this there were objections; and she finally resolved that it could be the

496
00:30:42,400 --> 00:30:46,565
last resource, if her private inquiries as to the absence of the family were unfavourably

497
00:30:46,565 --> 00:30:47,565
answered.

498
00:30:47,565 --> 00:30:51,433
Accordingly, when she retired at night, she asked the chambermaid whether Pemberley were

499
00:30:51,433 --> 00:30:56,500
not a very fine place, what was the name of its proprietor, and, with no little alarm,

500
00:30:56,500 --> 00:30:58,633
whether the family were down for the summer?

501
00:30:58,633 --> 00:31:03,466
A most welcome negative followed the last question; and her alarms being now removed,

502
00:31:03,466 --> 00:31:07,400
she was at leisure to feel a great deal of curiosity to see the house herself; and when

503
00:31:07,400 --> 00:31:11,299
the subject was revived the next morning, and she was again applied to, could readily

504
00:31:11,299 --> 00:31:15,900
answer, and with a proper air of indifference, that she had not really any dislike to the

505
00:31:15,900 --> 00:31:16,900
scheme.

506
00:31:16,900 --> 00:31:19,633
To Pemberley, therefore, they were to go.
